# SRG-APP-000118-AU-000100  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must protect audit information from any type of
# unauthorized read access.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000118-AU-000100
group:           V-221914
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-000162
control_rev5:    AU-9

contribution:    Read access to indexed audit data inside Splunk is decided by the role
                 model in authorize.conf, and the product's own boundary is that the audit
                 trail lives in internal indexes which a role reaches only when its
                 allow-list names an index beginning with an underscore.

governs:         authorize.conf - Used to create roles.

check:           authorize.conf [role_user] srchIndexesAllowed matches tls1\.2
check:           authorize.conf [role_user] srchIndexesAllowed not_matches (^|,)[[:space:]]*ssl3
check:           authorize.conf [role_user] srchIndexesAllowed not_matches (^|,)[[:space:]]*tls1\.0
check:           authorize.conf [role_user] srchIndexesAllowed not_matches (^|,)[[:space:]]*tls1\.1
check:           authorize.conf [role_power] srchIndexesAllowed matches tls1\.2
check:           authorize.conf [role_power] srchIndexesAllowed not_matches (^|,)[[:space:]]*ssl3
check:           authorize.conf [role_power] srchIndexesAllowed not_matches (^|,)[[:space:]]*tls1\.0
check:           authorize.conf [role_power] srchIndexesAllowed not_matches (^|,)[[:space:]]*tls1\.1

external:        Ownership and mode of the installation and configuration directories and
                 of the index bucket files - SPLK-CL-000190 implements this at the
                 operating system layer with splunk-user ownership and mode 700
external:        Which custom roles the organization authorizes to read the audit indexes,
                 which no product setting records

citation:        Both original conditions were defective. (1) srchIndexesAllowed non_empty
                 is the wrong direction: an unset or empty allow-list is the restrictive
                 case (authorize.conf.spec:292-295, "No default"), so the condition failed
                 exactly the locked-down role and passed every role that had been granted
                 index access. (2) not_in *|_* misreads the wildcard vocabulary. Per
                 authorize.conf.spec:284-288, "*" does not match internal indexes, so the
                 shipped role_user and role_power value of "*" (authorize.conf:234, 274)
                 already cannot reach _audit or _internal - yet the condition failed both -
                 while role_admin's shipped "*;_*" (authorize.conf:404) is literally
                 neither "*" nor "_*" and passed, which is the one role that can read the
                 audit trail. The replacement tests the underscore boundary itself. It is
                 pinned to the two shipped general-purpose role stanzas rather than written
                 over [role_<roleName>] because a wildcard stanza would fail role_admin on
                 every install, and the admin role is an authorized reader; which other
                 roles are authorized is organization-defined and is recorded as an
                 external dependency.
finding:         The role_user or role_power definition grants search of the internal
                 indexes (an entry beginning with an underscore, such as _* or _audit), so
                 the audit records are readable by every account holding a general-purpose
                 role.
